## Success metrics

| Metric | Target direction |
|---|---|
| Time to first local CRUD app | Lower than current WatermelonDB setup friction documented in installation/setup guides.[4][6] |
| Time to first sync integration | Lower than current custom integration burden implied by sync docs.[2][3] |
| Cold start performance at large local datasets | Equal to or better than WatermelonDB’s large-data positioning.[1] |
| Type coverage | Public APIs fully typed with stable inference |
| Migration success rate | High success across pilot migrations from WatermelonDB |
| Sync reliability | High completion and recovery success under network interruption.[3] |
| Documentation effectiveness | High task completion for setup, CRUD, sync, and migration guides |

## Open questions
- Which parts of existing WatermelonDB API compatibility are essential for adoption versus not worth preserving?
- Should the first web adapter target IndexedDB, SQLite via WASM, or remain experimental?
- Should sync checkpoints use timestamps only for v1, or support opaque cursors from day one?
- Which reference backend should be treated as the canonical example: Node/Postgres or Supabase/Postgres?[7][3]
- Should code generation be required for optimal typing, or optional for teams preferring pure code-first inference?

## Recommended next step
The immediate next step is to turn this PRD into an implementation program with RFCs for schema design, adapter contracts, sync protocol contracts, and React hook ergonomics. That work should begin with a compatibility matrix against current WatermelonDB setup, CRUD, and sync expectations so the successor preserves the right behaviors while modernizing the right abstractions.[4][5][2]